WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.287 --> 00:00:04.811
- [David] For 24 years,
I was an investigator

00:00:04.811 --> 00:00:06.811
for the IRS and the FBI.

00:00:09.002 --> 00:00:11.249
I started off working bank robbery cases.

00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:15.416
I ended up in Chicago and worked
political corruption cases.

00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:17.263
And after I retired,

00:00:17.263 --> 00:00:21.013
I started investigating
lost apple varieties.

00:00:26.782 --> 00:00:30.681
In the late 1800s, apples
were extremely important

00:00:30.681 --> 00:00:33.348
to early settlers in Washington.

00:00:34.676 --> 00:00:37.036
They grew a variety of apples.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:40.377
Most of those people decided
that farming was too hard

00:00:40.377 --> 00:00:42.986
but all over eastern Washington,

00:00:42.986 --> 00:00:46.319
those old orchards survived to this day.

00:00:47.362 --> 00:00:50.206
Sometimes, I'll just drive country roads

00:00:50.206 --> 00:00:53.192
and I'll look out and I can
recognize an apple tree from,

00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:56.264
you know, a couple hundred yards away.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:58.033
I wonder what's planted there.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.437
It's very possible it could be

00:00:59.437 --> 00:01:00.943
one of these lost varieties of apple

00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:03.026
just waiting to be found.

00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:10.915
I draw on my experience as an investigator

00:01:10.915 --> 00:01:13.748
mainly through the use of records.

00:01:14.628 --> 00:01:18.795
I'm searching for clues of
where these lost varieties grew.

00:01:19.402 --> 00:01:22.045
I look at plat maps, I
look through museum records

00:01:22.045 --> 00:01:23.795
and things like that.

00:01:27.647 --> 00:01:29.845
Then I go out just like
in law enforcement,

00:01:29.845 --> 00:01:32.036
I'm looking for physical evidence.

00:01:32.036 --> 00:01:35.040
And I've got to take a
look at those apples.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.716
I make a map and record
the GPS coordinates.

00:01:38.716 --> 00:01:40.664
I bag them up and I mail them off

00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:44.107
to apple identification experts.

00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:46.354
I'm collecting these apples

00:01:46.354 --> 00:01:49.576
from the only known Dickinson apple tree

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.000
in the United States.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.990
These apples are absolutely
delicious off the tree.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.044
There's only about 10 of us now

00:01:56.044 --> 00:02:00.211
that have ever tasted the
Dickinson in the last 100 years.

00:02:04.544 --> 00:02:07.157
I began searching for lost apple varieties

00:02:07.157 --> 00:02:10.079
probably about eight, nine years ago.

00:02:10.079 --> 00:02:11.746
Something like that.

00:02:13.163 --> 00:02:16.320
People often ask me why do
you look for these apples?

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.373
And I tell them that I have been concerned

00:02:18.373 --> 00:02:21.005
that if I don't look for
them, no one else will,

00:02:21.005 --> 00:02:24.505
and these varieties could be lost forever.

00:02:26.603 --> 00:02:30.754
So far, I found three
lost varieties of apples.

00:02:30.754 --> 00:02:32.967
Those varieties include the Nero

00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:36.579
and then the Arkansas
Beauty and the Dickinson.

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:38.977
So we're down to about 20 varieties

00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:40.514
that we're looking for right now

00:02:40.514 --> 00:02:44.681
and it's just a matter of
time hopefully to find those.

